{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"chrono","title":"Time with <chrono>","group":"Errors, Streams & Utilities","order":68,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>&lt;chrono&gt;</code> models time with types: <em>durations</em> (<code>5s</code>, <code>250ms</code>), <em>time points</em> (a clock reading) and <em>clocks</em>. Units are checked at compile time — no more “was that seconds or milliseconds?”.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hourglass\"></i>Durations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    using namespace std::chrono_literals;\n    using namespace std::chrono;\n\n    auto timeout = 2s + 500ms;                         // std::chrono::milliseconds (2500)\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; timeout.count() &lt;&lt; \"ms\\n\";\n\n    seconds s = duration_cast&lt;seconds&gt;(timeout);        // lossy conversion must be explicit\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; s.count() &lt;&lt; \"s\\n\";\n\n    minutes m = 90min;\n    hours h = duration_cast&lt;hours&gt;(m);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; h.count() &lt;&lt; \"h \" &lt;&lt; (m - h).count() &lt;&lt; \"min\\n\";\n\n    duration&lt;double&gt; secs = timeout;                    // floating-point seconds: implicit is fine\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; secs.count() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (1h &gt; 59min) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2500ms\n2s\n1h 30min\n2.5\n1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stopwatch\"></i>Measuring elapsed time</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;numeric&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();       // monotonic: right clock for measuring\n    std::vector&lt;long long&gt; v(1'000'000);\n    std::iota(v.begin(), v.end(), 1);\n    long long sum = std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0LL);\n    auto end = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();\n\n    auto us = std::chrono::duration_cast&lt;std::chrono::microseconds&gt;(end - start);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"sum = \" &lt;&lt; sum &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"took \" &lt;&lt; us.count() &lt;&lt; \" us\\n\";      // varies run to run\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Clock</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>steady_clock</code></td><td>Measuring intervals (never goes backwards)</td></tr><tr><td><code>system_clock</code></td><td>Wall-clock time; convertible to calendar dates / <code>time_t</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>high_resolution_clock</code></td><td>Alias of one of the above — prefer <code>steady_clock</code> explicitly</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar\"></i>Calendar dates (C++20)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;\n#include &lt;format&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    using namespace std::chrono;\n    year_month_day launch = 2024y / March / 15;\n    sys_days d = launch;\n    sys_days later = d + days(30);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(\"{} -&gt; {}\\n\", launch, year_month_day{later});\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; weekday{d} &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (2024y).is_leap() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::format(\"{}\\n\", 1500ms);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2024-03-15 -&gt; 2024-04-14\nFri 1\n1500ms</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why use std::chrono instead of plain integers for time?</summary><div><p>The unit is part of the type, so <code>sleep_for(5)</code> doesn't compile (5 what?) but <code>sleep_for(5s)</code> does, and conversions between units are exact and automatic when lossless (<code>2s</code> → <code>2000ms</code>) but must be explicit when lossy (<code>duration_cast</code>). That eliminates a whole class of unit bugs at zero run-time cost.</p></div></details></section>"}